\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Correct linear outcome mechanism, Bernoulli(.5) assignment. Left: increasing degree at fixed $n=1000$ inflates contrast variance; adding unnecessary quadratic or two-hop features further increases risk even though the models contain the truth. The dotted line is $\sqrt  {\mathbb  E[\|w\|^2]}$ for Add, not a fitted trend. Right: larger $n$ reduces Add risk at each fixed degree. Points summarize three fixed graph seeds; bars are approximate 95\% assignment/noise Monte Carlo intervals, not graph-population intervals.}}{5}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:degree}{{1}{5}{Correct linear outcome mechanism, Bernoulli(.5) assignment. Left: increasing degree at fixed $n=1000$ inflates contrast variance; adding unnecessary quadratic or two-hop features further increases risk even though the models contain the truth. The dotted line is $\sqrt {\E [\|w\|^2]}$ for Add, not a fitted trend. Right: larger $n$ reduces Add risk at each fixed degree. Points summarize three fixed graph seeds; bars are approximate 95\% assignment/noise Monte Carlo intervals, not graph-population intervals}{figure.1}{}}
